course: books
lesson: 72
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XII, part 3: The Return of Ulysses (the invitations, «SPEECH BY TOAD» and «not one little song»)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Next morning Toad oversleeps again and finds only egg-shells, cold leathery toast and a coffee-pot three-quarters empty — in his own house! Badger tells him there must be a Banquet to celebrate, and Toad must spend the morning writing the invitations. Toad starts to refuse, then suddenly agrees with a grand speech about sacrificing «this fair morning on the altar of duty and friendship».
explain: Why so willing? Toad has had «a fine idea». In every invitation he boasts about how he flattened the Chief Weasel, and he adds a programme for the evening: SPEECH BY TOAD, ADDRESS BY TOAD, SONG BY TOAD, OTHER COMPOSITIONS BY TOAD. By noon they are done, and a timid, bedraggled weasel, one of last night's prisoners, is sent off to deliver them.
explain: At lunch Toad is so puffed up that the others suspect something. Afterwards the Rat and Badger take him by both arms into the smoking-room. The Rat says it kindly but firmly: there will be no speeches and no songs. «We're not arguing with you; we're just telling you.» «Mayn't I sing them just one little song?» «No, not one little song.» Toad's songs are conceit and boasting — «And gas,» adds Badger.
explain: Toad thinks a long time and then says sadly: «You have conquered, my friends... Henceforth I will be a very different Toad... But, O dear, O dear, this is a hard world!» He leaves, pressing his handkerchief to his face. «I feel like a brute,» says the Rat. But Badger explains: Toad has to live here and be respected, not be a laughing-stock for the weasels. And the Rat reveals he caught the invitations in time — Mole is now writing plain, simple ones in the blue boudoir (a small, pretty sitting-room).
fact: Toad «came down to breakfast disgracefully late» and found «a certain quantity of egg-shells, some fragments of cold and leathery toast, a coffee-pot three-fourths empty».
fact: Mole and the Rat were out on the lawn «roaring with laughter and kicking their short legs up in the air», evidently telling each other stories; Toad decided «he would get square with the others sooner or later» (get square = get even).
fact: Badger: «we really ought to have a Banquet at once, to celebrate this affair. It's expected of you—in fact, it's the rule.» Badger himself will order the Banquet; Toad must write the invitations.
fact: Toad tries «Me stop indoors and write a lot of rotten letters on a jolly morning like this...» then turns: «I sacrifice this fair morning on the altar of duty and friendship!»
fact: «The Badger looked at him very suspiciously, but Toad's frank, open countenance made it difficult to suggest any unworthy motive» (countenance = face; motive = reason for doing something).
fact: The letter-paper had «'Toad Hall' at the top in blue and gold».
fact: Toad's programme: «SPEECH. . . . BY TOAD. (There will be other speeches by TOAD during the evening.)», then «ADDRESS. . . BY TOAD», «SONG. . . . BY TOAD. (Composed by himself.)», and «OTHER COMPOSITIONS. BY TOAD will be sung in the course of the evening by the. . . COMPOSER.»
fact: The Address had six parts: «Our Prison System—the Waterways of Old England—Horse-dealing, and how to deal—Property, its rights and its duties—Back to the Land—A Typical English Squire.» (a squire = a country gentleman who owns land)
fact: The messenger was «a small and rather bedraggled weasel», one of the prisoners, «very respectful and anxious to please»; Toad told him there «might be a shilling for him, or, again, perhaps there mightn't».
fact: At lunch Toad was «so uppish and inflated» that Mole began to suspect something, and the Rat and Badger «exchanged significant glances» (uppish = too pleased with himself).
fact: The Rat: «there are going to be no speeches and no songs. Try and grasp the fact that on this occasion we're not arguing with you; we're just telling you.»
fact: «Mayn't I sing them just one little song?» he pleaded piteously. «No, not one little song,» replied the Rat firmly, though his heart bled.
fact: The Rat: «your songs are all conceit and boasting and vanity; and your speeches are all self-praise and... gross exaggeration»; Badger: «And gas» (gas = empty boastful talk).
fact: Toad: «It was, to be sure, but a small thing that I asked—merely leave to blossom and expand for yet one more evening... But, O dear, O dear, this is a hard world!»
fact: Badger: «This good fellow has got to live here, and hold his own, and be respected. Would you have him a common laughing-stock, mocked and jeered at by stoats and weasels?» The Rat had read the invitations: «simply disgraceful», and «confiscated the lot» (took them all away).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «extra» = over the top; «no cap» = honestly; «lush» (Wales) = lovely; «eejit» (Ireland) = idiot; «yinz» (Pittsburgh, US) = you all.
exercise: Why did Toad find almost nothing left at breakfast? | He had overslept, as usual | The weasels ate it | Badger hid it | There was no food left
exercise: How much coffee was left in the pot? | About a quarter | None at all | Half | It was full and still hot
exercise: What were Mole and the Rat doing on the lawn? | Telling stories, roaring with laughter | Fighting | Rowing | Mending the broken windows
exercise: «He would get square with the others sooner or later.» What does «get square» mean? | Get even | Get tidy again | Draw a square | Make friends again
exercise: Why, says Badger, must there be a Banquet? | It is expected — it's the rule | Toad wants one | The weasels asked for one | It is Toad's birthday
exercise: What job did Badger give Toad? | Writing the invitations | Cooking | Cleaning the windows | Ordering the food from the town
exercise: What did Badger say he would do himself? | Order the Banquet | Write letters | Sweep | Go fishing
exercise: «I sacrifice this fair morning on the altar of duty and friendship!» What is Toad really up to? | He has a secret plan for the invitations | He is truly being unselfish, as a good host should | He is joking | He is going to church
exercise: Why was it hard for Badger to accuse Toad? | Toad's «frank, open countenance» | Toad was crying | Badger was busy in the kitchen | Toad had a letter
exercise: What was printed at the top of the letter-paper? | «Toad Hall» in blue and gold | A crown | A motor-car | A picture of a toad
exercise: What did Toad plan to boast about in the letters? | How he had laid the Chief Weasel flat | His washing on the barge | His father's money and his fine old house | His boat
exercise: How many items of the programme were «BY TOAD»? | All of them | Only the first one | Just two of them | Half of them, the other half by Badger
exercise: What was NOT one of the subjects of Toad's Address? | How to drive a motor-car | Our Prison System | Horse-dealing, and how to deal | A Typical English Squire
exercise: Who delivered the invitations? | A weasel prisoner | Mole, the next morning | The postman from the village with his bag | Otter, from the river
exercise: What did Toad promise the weasel messenger? | Perhaps a shilling, or perhaps not | Five pounds | A job as a footman at Toad Hall | Nothing at all, not even thanks
exercise: How did Toad behave at lunch? | Uppish and inflated | Sad and quiet, hardly eating anything | Sleepy | Kind and humble
exercise: What did Rat and Badger do after lunch? | Took Toad by both arms into the smoking-room | Went fishing | Wrote letters | Locked Toad in his bedroom
exercise: «We're not arguing with you; we're just telling you.» What were they telling him? | No speeches, no songs | No breakfast | No more cars, boats or caravans ever again | No visitors
exercise: «Mayn't I sing them just one little song?» What was the answer? | «No, not one little song» | «Just one» | «Two, if they are very short and quiet ones» | «Only the Ducks' Ditty»
exercise: «And gas.» What does Badger mean by «gas»? | Empty boastful talk | Petrol | Air | Cooking gas
exercise: «You have conquered, my friends.» What does Toad mean? | He gives in and agrees | He won the fight | He is angry | He is going to sing anyway
exercise: How did the Rat feel after the talk? | Like a brute | Proud of a job well done | Happy and relieved | Hungry and cross
exercise: Why, says Badger, did it have to be done? | So Toad would be respected | To punish him | To save money on food and wine for the guests | To please the weasels
exercise: What had the Rat done with Toad's invitations? | Took them all away | Posted them | Burnt them in the kitchen fire | Framed them
exercise: Who was writing the new invitations? | Mole, in the blue boudoir | Badger | The Rat himself, in the smoking-room | The weasel
test: Ruby: «Toad wrote the invites 'cos he's a proper team player now, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | He had a plan to boast in them | Yes, he wanted to help | He refused and Mole wrote them all | Badger wrote them
test: Why does Badger look at Toad «very suspiciously»? | Toad agrees much too quickly | Toad is crying | Toad has ink all over his nose and paws | Toad is late
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Toad's programme had speeches by many animals.» | False: every item was by Toad | True: Badger and Rat spoke too | True: Mole sang | False: there was no programme
test: Why is «OTHER COMPOSITIONS BY TOAD will be sung by the COMPOSER» funny? | The composer is Toad too | Toad cannot sing | The composer is Badger, who writes the tunes | It means a band
test: Leah, «that's lush» (lovely): «Rat enjoyed telling Toad off, right?» What does the book say? | His heart bled | He loved every bit | He laughed out loud | He didn't care at all
test: What would have happened if Toad's invitations had gone out? | The guests would have laughed at him | Everyone would admire him | Nobody would come, because of the rain | The police would come
test: Why does Badger call Toad «this good fellow» even after the trouble? | He sees good in Toad | He is joking | He forgot all about last night's trouble | He is being sarcastic
test: Sean, «don't be an eejit (idiot)»: «Toad cried 'cos Rat hit him.» Put it right. | He was sad about no songs | Yes, Rat hit him | He had a cold from the river | He lost money
test: Looking back: in chapter X, what did Toad do when he sang his conceited song on the road? | Met the owners of the stolen car | Won a prize | Made friends with everyone on the road | Hid quietly
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, how did Toad behave after Badger's talk in the smoking-room? | Outside, he said he wasn't sorry | He kept his promise to Badger | He sang | He left home
test: Looking back: in chapter II, Toad said «'Live for others!' That's my motto in life.» How does that compare with his words about «duty and friendship» now? | Both hide a plan | Both were true | Both were said to Mole | Both were songs
test: Looking back: in chapter XI, why did Toad's father keep the secret passage from him? | Toad «cannot hold his tongue» | Toad was a baby | The passage was dangerous and might fall in | Toad hated tunnels
test: Looking back: which animal did Toad send off with the invitations, and what had that animal done the night before? | A weasel prisoner | Mole, who had fought | Otter, who was a sweep | A stoat sentry who fled
test: Ella: «Badger and Rat are just mean, they want Toad to be boring.» Best answer from the story? | They want him respected | Yes, they hate fun | They want his house | They want to sing themselves
test: How is this smoking-room talk different from the one in chapter VI? | This time Toad really gives in | It is exactly the same | Badger is not there this time | Toad wins
test: ★★★ The coffee-pot was «three-fourths empty». What fraction of the coffee was left for Toad? | One quarter | Three quarters | Exactly one third | About a half
test: ★★★ Toad's Address had six parts. If each part took 10 minutes, how long would the Address alone last? | An hour | Half an hour | Six minutes | Sixteen minutes
test: ★★★ «There will be other speeches by TOAD during the evening.» What is the trap in that line? | It promises even more Toad | It means other people will speak | It means Toad will be quiet | It is a mistake
exam: Identify the occupation assigned to Toad by the Badger upon the morning following the recapture of Toad Hall. | The writing of invitations to the Banquet | The cooking of the Banquet in the kitchens of the Hall | The cleaning of the banqueting-hall | The questioning of the prisoners
exam: Ascertain the true motive underlying Toad's sudden acquiescence in the Badger's request. | His design to fill the invitations with self-praise | His sincere devotion to duty and friendship | His fear of the Badger | His desire to remain indoors
exam: It is given that Toad was conducted to the smoking-room. State the prohibition there communicated to him. | That there were to be no speeches and no songs | That he was not to leave the Hall until the guests had departed | That he was not to eat | That he must not invite the Otter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Badger's justification for the said prohibition? | That Toad must be respected, not a laughing-stock | That speeches were contrary to law in the district | That the guests disliked music | That the Banquet had been cancelled
